## Explorer Session 2026-03-21

**Topic**: Self-directed — confronting the schizophrenia prediction with empirical neuroscience

**Key finding**: The prediction "Schizophrenia shows over-coherence (C > 0.80) in some circuits" is not flatly wrong but misses the real picture. The literature shows a paradoxical **dual pattern**:

- **Local hyperconnectivity**: elevated gamma synchrony in sensory circuits correlates with hallucination severity — this part the prediction captures
- **Global dysconnectivity**: reduced inter-regional functional connectivity — this the prediction ignores
- **E/I imbalance**: the "over-coherence" is excitatory noise (GABAergic interneuron dysfunction), not meaningful resonance

**Deeper insight**: This is the **consciousness analog of the three-C problem**. At galactic scales, C needs to be both density-based and acceleration-based. At neural scales, C needs to be both local and global. The same limitation — a single scalar can't capture multi-scale structure — appears at every scale the framework operates.

**The hopeful thread**: MRH, if formalized, could provide exactly the missing scale-dependent structure. Defining C_within_MRH vs C_across_MRH would transform vague predictions into testable ones.

